1
00:00:01,125 --> 00:00:02,251
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:02,376 --> 00:00:05,630
- You really like this guy, huh?
- He got a promotion in D.C.

3
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:07,965
- and he asked me to go with him.
- Yeah.

4
00:00:08,257 --> 00:00:11,135
- Have you told Jane you're leaving yet?
- No. No, not yet.

5
00:00:11,385 --> 00:00:13,179
I really want you
to be happy.

6
00:00:13,679 --> 00:00:15,515
And that is the most important thing
to me...

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:17,350
That you do what makes you happy.

8
00:00:17,725 --> 00:00:19,810
I wish we were
somewhere more romantic

9
00:00:19,936 --> 00:00:21,103
but what the hell?

10
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:22,605
Will you marry me?

11
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:24,941
Um, wow. Marry you?

12
00:00:26,067 --> 00:00:27,109
Um...

13
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:39,372
You're very chipper today.

14
00:00:39,789 --> 00:00:40,831
Why not?

15
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:42,917
I thought you'd be sad
about Lisbon leaving for D.C.

16
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:44,335
Oh, Lisbon's not leaving.

17
00:00:44,669 --> 00:00:47,004
She likes to talk like she might
but I don't think she's going anywhere.

18
00:00:47,505 --> 00:00:49,882
From what I heard, it's a done deal.
She put in her transfer papers.

19
00:00:50,258 --> 00:00:51,926
She starts work at the D.C.
office next month.

20
00:00:52,051 --> 00:00:53,719
She's leaving Austin in a week,
next Friday.

21
00:00:55,221 --> 00:00:57,390
Morning.
Captain Muller, campus police.

22
00:00:57,848 --> 00:00:58,891
Cho. This is Jane.

23
00:01:00,601 --> 00:01:01,644
This way, fellas.

24
00:01:09,694 --> 00:01:11,445
We got a call at 6:07 a.m.

25
00:01:12,488 --> 00:01:14,365
Housemates woke up and found him
like this.

26
00:01:17,368 --> 00:01:18,911
Name's Tyler Vankamp...

27
00:01:19,662 --> 00:01:20,871
Sophomore studying business.

28
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:24,250
- Practice-squad linebacker.
- You sure about Lisbon?

29
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:26,836
Did you hear it from her or did
you hear it from other people?

30
00:01:27,128 --> 00:01:28,170
Say?

31
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:29,380
That's okay, sir.
He's talking to me.

32
00:01:29,964 --> 00:01:31,257
I heard from other people...
Abbott.

33
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:34,051
Well, it's not a done deal, then, is it?
It's just a rumor.

34
00:01:34,343 --> 00:01:35,386
Jane, she's leaving.

35
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:37,638
I'll believe it when I hear it from her.

36
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:39,599
- Just telling you what I heard.
- Uh, excuse me?

37
00:01:40,099 --> 00:01:41,142
What?

38
00:01:41,392 --> 00:01:42,685
- Tyler Vankamp.
- Yeah, we're on it.

39
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:44,270
Where are these, uh, housemates?

40
00:01:44,854 --> 00:01:45,896
This way.

41
00:01:47,648 --> 00:01:48,691
Hello. Finally.

42
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:50,526
We've been sitting here
like two hours, dude.

43
00:01:50,651 --> 00:01:52,570
- You guys heard of human rights?
- No such thing.

44
00:02:11,589 --> 00:02:13,049
Which one of you found Tyler's body?

45
00:02:14,717 --> 00:02:16,218
- I did.
- What time was that?

46
00:02:17,553 --> 00:02:18,596
6:00 a.m.

47
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:20,014
What were you doing up
at 6:00 a.m.?

48
00:02:20,514 --> 00:02:21,891
Me and Colt go running.

49
00:02:28,522 --> 00:02:29,607
You guys have any playing cards?

50
00:02:30,149 --> 00:02:31,192
- No.
- Yes.

51
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:33,277
- No.
- Some... place.

52
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:38,783
Over there. First drawer.

53
00:02:47,458 --> 00:02:48,501
Aha.

54
00:02:52,672 --> 00:02:53,714
Yes.

55
00:02:54,757 --> 00:02:56,509
She's leaving a week from Friday?

56
00:02:57,301 --> 00:02:58,344
Yeah.

57
00:02:58,511 --> 00:03:00,221
- That's only 10 days.
- Yeah.

58
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,350
When was the last time
anyone saw Tyler alive?

59
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:08,104
I-I came in at like 2:00,
and he was up studying.

60
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:10,398
Right there on the couch, totally chill.

61
00:03:11,732 --> 00:03:13,859
We figured he must have surprised
a sneak thief.

62
00:03:14,235 --> 00:03:15,945
- We need to go, Cho.
- We're working.

63
00:03:16,404 --> 00:03:19,323
Uh, yeah. Sorry. This.
I almost forgot to say it.

64
00:03:19,615 --> 00:03:20,658
It was all of them.

65
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:22,368
The ninja knife wasn't in
more than an inch

66
00:03:22,493 --> 00:03:24,662
not deep enough to kill him,
so, uh, he was drowned.

67
00:03:24,954 --> 00:03:27,456
- In a kiddie pool?
- Well, if someone was helping him along.

68
00:03:27,581 --> 00:03:30,126
I mean, a big, athletic guy like Tyler

69
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:32,169
I would guess that that would be
a job for two people.

70
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:33,921
- Yeah, but why drown him?
- Well, probably something to do

71
00:03:34,046 --> 00:03:36,173
with the drunken card game
that took place here last night.

72
00:03:36,424 --> 00:03:38,801
They used toy money as chips.

73
00:03:39,009 --> 00:03:40,136
Tyler was a player, huh?

74
00:03:40,553 --> 00:03:43,013
Flashy gold watch, slick sneakers.

75
00:03:43,222 --> 00:03:45,141
I bet he knew how to play cards.

76
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:47,309
I bet he won a lotta money.

77
00:03:48,102 --> 00:03:49,562
Money that none of you could spare.

78
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:53,399
Then someone accused him
of cheating.

79
00:03:53,524 --> 00:03:56,068
That would be you with the red eyes
and the turtleneck

80
00:03:56,193 --> 00:03:57,945
to cover the bruises on your neck.

81
00:03:58,070 --> 00:03:59,488
- What's your name?
- Barry.

82
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:00,865
Barry.

83
00:04:01,365 --> 00:04:03,993
Tyler's an angry steroid freak.
He got mad.

84
00:04:04,118 --> 00:04:07,037
He smashed a chair
and started throttling you

85
00:04:07,163 --> 00:04:10,708
until, uh, this brave man
stepped in to help you

86
00:04:10,833 --> 00:04:13,127
and received a punch
in the face for his trouble.

87
00:04:13,711 --> 00:04:17,089
Hence the, uh, badly applied makeup
covering your black eye.

88
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:19,383
You applied the makeup.

89
00:04:21,677 --> 00:04:24,555
You wear your watch on your right hand,
probably left-handed.

90
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:27,308
So judging by the angle
and the depth of the wound

91
00:04:27,433 --> 00:04:29,769
I would guess that it was you
that stabbed Tyler in the back

92
00:04:29,894 --> 00:04:31,812
to stop him from throttling Barry here.

93
00:04:32,813 --> 00:04:34,690
Now, stabbing Tyler in the back
didn't help much.

94
00:04:34,815 --> 00:04:35,858
It made him madder.

95
00:04:35,983 --> 00:04:37,193
He chased you outside.
He fell in the pool.

96
00:04:37,318 --> 00:04:40,237
That's when you two
with the still-damp sneakers

97
00:04:40,529 --> 00:04:43,324
got on top of him and held him
down until he drowned.

98
00:04:43,824 --> 00:04:46,744
Then you all came inside
and cleaned the room.

99
00:04:47,244 --> 00:04:49,205
Now, what self-respecting student

100
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:52,374
voluntarily throws away
perfectly good vodka?

101
00:04:52,917 --> 00:04:54,543
Can we go now?
I have stuff to do.

102
00:04:56,128 --> 00:04:57,171
I guess so.

103
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:02,510
You're all under arrest.

104
00:05:15,395 --> 00:05:16,855
{\an8}- "Thank you."
- "You're so welcome."

105
00:05:17,147 --> 00:05:21,151
{\an8}- Oh, I'm gonna miss you so much.
- Me too. It was sweet.

106
00:05:23,445 --> 00:05:24,905
What's with all the hugging?
You leaving today?

107
00:05:26,073 --> 00:05:27,115
Tomorrow.

108
00:05:29,201 --> 00:05:30,619
{\an8}Are you still mad at me?

109
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:33,038
Do I look mad?

110
00:05:33,497 --> 00:05:34,540
Yeah, you do.

111
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,083
Well, maybe I am.

112
00:05:36,625 --> 00:05:40,420
{\an8}Jane, this is something I need to do.
Please, just be happy for me.

113
00:05:40,546 --> 00:05:42,172
{\an8}I am happy for you.

114
00:05:42,339 --> 00:05:44,800
{\an8}I'm just a little sad for me
because you're leaving.

115
00:05:45,259 --> 00:05:46,343
I'm not mad about it.

116
00:05:46,468 --> 00:05:49,054
I just feel a little slighted
because I was the last to know.

117
00:05:49,888 --> 00:05:51,932
{\an8}You know damn well
why I couldn't tell you.

118
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:54,226
It's hard. We've worked together
a long time.

119
00:05:55,644 --> 00:05:57,646
{\an8}So stay. Don't go.

120
00:05:59,189 --> 00:06:00,232
{\an8}Don't break up the team.

121
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:02,568
{\an8}I'm kidding. I'm kidding.

122
00:06:03,235 --> 00:06:04,278
{\an8}I'm happy for you.

123
00:06:04,945 --> 00:06:06,196
You know I'm happy for you.

124
00:06:07,489 --> 00:06:10,200
{\an8}You deserve this. You...
You deserve him.

125
00:06:11,618 --> 00:06:12,661
{\an8}He's a good man.

126
00:06:13,287 --> 00:06:14,329
Thank you.

127
00:06:15,122 --> 00:06:16,665
All right, people, gather up.

128
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:21,003
{\an8}This letter was sent to Chief Agent Shultz

129
00:06:21,211 --> 00:06:23,714
by U.S. mail, arriving an hour ago.

130
00:06:26,008 --> 00:06:28,677
{\an8}"Remember Greta De Jorio?
That was me."

131
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:31,138
"To honor Greta's memory"

132
00:06:31,263 --> 00:06:35,100
"I will start killing again
very soon and very publicly."

133
00:06:35,851 --> 00:06:38,937
"And you FBI simpletons
still won't catch me."

134
00:06:39,855 --> 00:06:42,065
"Ask yourselves...
do you understand"

135
00:06:42,566 --> 00:06:47,863
{\an8}"752 598
H is M Key O?"

136
00:06:49,031 --> 00:06:50,073
Interesting.

137
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,202
Greta De Jorio was murdered
five years ago

138
00:06:54,328 --> 00:06:55,871
in Miami Beach, Florida

139
00:06:55,996 --> 00:06:57,664
{\an8}and the FBI team down there
worked the case hard

140
00:06:57,789 --> 00:06:59,416
{\an8}but nobody was ever charged.

141
00:06:59,708 --> 00:07:00,834
She was a prominent socialite

142
00:07:00,959 --> 00:07:03,587
so the Bureau got beat up a lot
in the press at the time.

143
00:07:04,296 --> 00:07:07,382
De Jorio's body was found
in the trunk of her burnt-out car

144
00:07:07,507 --> 00:07:09,301
on a remote dirt road
in the everglades.

145
00:07:09,635 --> 00:07:11,970
Why was the letter
sent to Austin, not Miami?

146
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:15,265
{\an8}We assume it's because the chief
was running the Miami office

147
00:07:15,390 --> 00:07:16,433
{\an8}of the Bureau at the time.

148
00:07:16,558 --> 00:07:17,935
Perhaps this guy has a grudge
against her.

149
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:20,187
There's nothing in the letter to prove
it's from the actual killer.

150
00:07:20,479 --> 00:07:22,773
{\an8}That's true.
But if this unsub kills again

151
00:07:22,898 --> 00:07:26,902
{\an8}and brags about outwitting the Bureau
it could be a PR nightmare.

152
00:07:27,694 --> 00:07:30,822
{\an8}The chief is throwing everything
that we've got at this.

153
00:07:31,323 --> 00:07:33,700
Leave and transfers are canceled.

154
00:07:34,534 --> 00:07:36,912
{\an8}Everybody that is here now
is working on this case

155
00:07:37,537 --> 00:07:39,831
{\an8}and no other until we get results.

156
00:07:40,332 --> 00:07:43,543
{\an8}Boss, no transfers? I-I'm supposed
to leave for D.C. tomorrow.

157
00:07:44,086 --> 00:07:45,796
I'm sorry, Teresa.
No exceptions.

158
00:07:46,046 --> 00:07:50,008
{\an8}Cho, Fischer, get a team together,
head down to Miami.

159
00:07:50,133 --> 00:07:53,553
{\an8}Start by finding and debriefing
all of the original investigators.

160
00:07:53,929 --> 00:07:57,933
{\an8}Okay? Jane, any read on this code
or whatever it is?

161
00:07:59,059 --> 00:08:00,102
Nope.

162
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:04,564
All right.
Wylie, start crunching that.

163
00:08:06,024 --> 00:08:08,360
{\an8}Jane, a moment in my office, please.

164
00:08:11,196 --> 00:08:12,990
{\an8}We'll close this one quickly.
He wants to be caught.

165
00:08:13,115 --> 00:08:15,367
{\an8}Besides, we get to go to Miami.
That's a fun place, right?

166
00:08:15,575 --> 00:08:16,618
{\an8}I guess.

167
00:08:17,744 --> 00:08:18,787
So cheer up.

168
00:08:19,955 --> 00:08:22,249
{\an8}This is gonna be our last case together.
Let's make it a happy one.

169
00:08:22,416 --> 00:08:23,458
It's a murder case.

170
00:08:24,251 --> 00:08:27,963
{\an8}Well... Yeah.
We've had fun before, haven't we?

171
00:08:30,132 --> 00:08:31,299
{\an8}You know what? You're right.

172
00:08:31,758 --> 00:08:32,801
I'll cheer up.

173
00:08:33,427 --> 00:08:34,469
Great.

174
00:08:35,429 --> 00:08:36,513
- Good.
- Okay.

175
00:08:47,774 --> 00:08:48,817
You're acting weird.

176
00:08:49,943 --> 00:08:51,403
- Weird?
- Yeah.

177
00:08:53,113 --> 00:08:56,825
{\an8}You're acting cool about stuff
that I know you can't be cool with.

178
00:08:57,951 --> 00:08:58,994
Anything you wanna tell me?

179
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:02,122
No.

180
00:09:03,874 --> 00:09:04,916
All right.

181
00:10:07,687 --> 00:10:08,730
{\an8}Wow, huh?

182
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:12,025
- "Look at those sailboats."
- "Yep, it's beautiful."

183
00:10:12,192 --> 00:10:14,361
Yeah. Yeah.
Pelicans, too.

184
00:10:15,362 --> 00:10:18,323
Yeah, you won't see a view like this
behind a desk in D.C. though, huh?

185
00:10:18,865 --> 00:10:21,326
This is the third time today
you've said something like this.

186
00:10:21,451 --> 00:10:24,579
And you're correct. There are
no pelicans or sailboats in D.C.

187
00:10:24,704 --> 00:10:25,747
but I'll manage somehow.

188
00:10:26,623 --> 00:10:27,666
Yeah, I'm just saying.

189
00:10:27,958 --> 00:10:30,001
"Yeah, yeah. No."

190
00:10:31,211 --> 00:10:32,671
Uh, look, I don't care.
Just do it.

191
00:10:34,548 --> 00:10:36,508
Hi. Don De Jorio.

192
00:10:37,008 --> 00:10:38,718
FBI, huh?
Okay.

193
00:10:39,761 --> 00:10:41,388
I got 10 minutes. Shoot.

194
00:10:42,055 --> 00:10:44,266
There's been a development
in your wife's case.

195
00:10:45,851 --> 00:10:47,018
Oh, this is Megan, by the way.

196
00:10:47,435 --> 00:10:48,478
Hi.

197
00:10:48,770 --> 00:10:50,522
- Wife number two?
- Number three, actually.

198
00:10:51,356 --> 00:10:52,440
But who's counting?

199
00:10:53,692 --> 00:10:55,652
And this is my lawyer, Ted Randolph.

200
00:10:55,777 --> 00:10:57,028
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

201
00:10:57,195 --> 00:10:58,738
He's here because last time 'round

202
00:10:58,864 --> 00:11:01,241
you federal geniuses
tried to pin this murder on me.

203
00:11:01,408 --> 00:11:03,493
Now, is this more
pointless harassment

204
00:11:03,618 --> 00:11:05,245
or did you actually catch somebody?

205
00:11:05,495 --> 00:11:08,248
- No, sir.
- We received a letter from the killer.

206
00:11:09,749 --> 00:11:11,501
It says he's gonna kill again.

207
00:11:11,668 --> 00:11:14,004
W-what does that have to do
with Mr. De Jorio?

208
00:11:14,421 --> 00:11:17,299
He was thoroughly investigated
and conclusively exonerated.

209
00:11:17,465 --> 00:11:18,508
Not by me.

210
00:11:19,551 --> 00:11:21,428
I'm kidding. That was a little joke.

211
00:11:23,013 --> 00:11:24,556
Actually, I don't think
that you killed your wife.

212
00:11:24,681 --> 00:11:26,183
- I have a different theory.
- Yeah? What's that?

213
00:11:28,101 --> 00:11:29,144
Hey, there.

214
00:11:30,187 --> 00:11:31,229
Come on in.

215
00:11:31,688 --> 00:11:33,815
Oh. Chris. Christie, come here.

216
00:11:37,777 --> 00:11:39,112
This is my daughter, Christie.

217
00:11:39,446 --> 00:11:41,239
Chris, these people are here
about your mother.

218
00:11:41,364 --> 00:11:43,158
Some wacko sent a letter.

219
00:11:43,408 --> 00:11:44,993
Hi, Christie. Nice to meet you.

220
00:11:47,245 --> 00:11:48,413
Now, what's this theory of yours?

221
00:11:49,122 --> 00:11:51,666
I think this letter
wasn't written by the killer.

222
00:11:52,209 --> 00:11:54,586
I think this letter was written
by someone

223
00:11:54,711 --> 00:11:56,254
who thinks they know who the killer is

224
00:11:56,379 --> 00:11:57,881
someone who wants revenge...

225
00:11:58,632 --> 00:12:01,968
- Someone devilishly clever and patient.
- Really? Why?

226
00:12:02,260 --> 00:12:04,930
Well, imagine if you're the killer
and you read this letter.

227
00:12:05,055 --> 00:12:06,431
It would freak you out, wouldn't it?

228
00:12:06,806 --> 00:12:09,017
Here's the FBI.
They're on your trail again.

229
00:12:09,267 --> 00:12:12,062
Why? What's it all about, huh?

230
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:14,814
Why are they pretending to be you?
Why now?

231
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:16,107
What does this code mean?

232
00:12:16,608 --> 00:12:19,653
It's possible, or it was sent
by the actual killer.

233
00:12:19,861 --> 00:12:21,154
Don't waste your time.

234
00:12:21,279 --> 00:12:24,366
The killer is Wes Baxter,
like I've always said.

235
00:12:24,491 --> 00:12:27,035
Don, let's stick to the essentials here.

236
00:12:27,244 --> 00:12:29,287
Wes Baxter. He did it.

237
00:12:29,454 --> 00:12:33,041
Wes Baxter was a prime suspect
but he was cleared in the investigation.

238
00:12:33,291 --> 00:12:34,876
They just couldn't nail him.

239
00:12:35,335 --> 00:12:36,628
He lived next door.

240
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:40,215
The little creep was always
spying on Greta, stalking her.

241
00:12:40,674 --> 00:12:42,425
- It was him!
- Christie.

242
00:12:43,885 --> 00:12:45,345
What do you think of Wes Baxter?

243
00:12:46,012 --> 00:12:48,640
- It doesn't matter what I think.
- It matters a lot.

244
00:12:49,557 --> 00:12:50,600
Tell us.

245
00:12:51,434 --> 00:12:52,769
He was okay. I liked him.

246
00:12:52,978 --> 00:12:54,229
- Chris.
- She's speaking.

247
00:12:55,188 --> 00:12:56,231
He didn't kill Mom.

248
00:12:57,732 --> 00:13:01,486
You know, Chris has been through
a lot, all right?

249
00:13:01,611 --> 00:13:03,196
Now, she doesn't remember
everything so clearly

250
00:13:03,321 --> 00:13:05,490
- so why don't you just leave her out of it?
- No, Dad, I remember.

251
00:13:05,615 --> 00:13:08,368
You hounded Wes like you know
he did it, but you don't know.

252
00:13:08,493 --> 00:13:10,412
Christie, you don't have to get upset.

253
00:13:10,787 --> 00:13:12,580
I'm upset because you're being a jerk.

254
00:13:12,706 --> 00:13:13,999
Excuse me, young lady.

255
00:13:14,457 --> 00:13:16,835
That is no way to talk to your father
in front of company.

256
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:19,379
- Oh, you be quiet, you.
- Excuse me?

257
00:13:20,255 --> 00:13:22,048
No, Megan, I don't excuse you.

258
00:13:22,465 --> 00:13:23,717
You're a witch, and I hate you.

259
00:13:25,093 --> 00:13:28,305
That is beyond rude
and completely unacceptable!

260
00:13:28,555 --> 00:13:29,764
I am talking to you!

261
00:13:30,974 --> 00:13:33,143
Hello? Are you gonna let her
treat me like that?

262
00:13:33,435 --> 00:13:34,853
- We're gonna get going.
- Yeah.

263
00:13:35,770 --> 00:13:36,813
Excuse me.

264
00:13:39,733 --> 00:13:41,109
- You gonna let her treat me...
- All right, all right, all right.

265
00:13:41,234 --> 00:13:42,277
Come, come.

266
00:13:42,527 --> 00:13:45,363
I want her out of this house. You need
to buy her that condo so she leaves.

267
00:13:45,488 --> 00:13:46,531
"She's 19."

268
00:13:47,157 --> 00:13:49,743
- We need to talk to Wes Baxter.
- Okay.

269
00:13:51,036 --> 00:13:52,662
Married life, huh? Whoa.

270
00:13:53,663 --> 00:13:54,706
Yep.

271
00:13:54,831 --> 00:13:57,208
I mean no disrespect to your marriage.

272
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,752
I'm not getting married.

273
00:13:58,918 --> 00:14:01,254
Well, you're moving
to a new state with a man.

274
00:14:01,379 --> 00:14:04,132
Basically, you're getting married,
in effect.

275
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:06,092
I mean, lucky you've had
all that time to get to know him...

276
00:14:06,676 --> 00:14:07,886
Weeks and weeks.

277
00:14:09,179 --> 00:14:10,555
- Months.
- A month, even.

278
00:14:12,307 --> 00:14:13,850
I was thinking
about what you were saying

279
00:14:13,975 --> 00:14:16,061
about the letter not being sent
from the killer.

280
00:14:16,311 --> 00:14:18,104
If you're right, maybe it was
the daughter.

281
00:14:18,229 --> 00:14:19,647
There was a five-year gap.

282
00:14:19,773 --> 00:14:21,775
She would've been 14
at the time of the murder...

283
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:23,526
Too young to take action until now.

284
00:14:24,027 --> 00:14:25,653
That's a great idea.
Yeah.

285
00:14:26,071 --> 00:14:27,655
Could be her. Um...

286
00:14:28,323 --> 00:14:29,991
What do you think this code means?

287
00:14:30,116 --> 00:14:33,203
I don't know.
If you can't solve it, I definitely can't.

288
00:14:33,328 --> 00:14:35,830
Well, I think if we put our heads together
we can solve it.

289
00:14:36,122 --> 00:14:39,667
"752 598 H is M Key O."

290
00:14:40,251 --> 00:14:41,294
It's not a phone number.

291
00:14:42,462 --> 00:14:44,756
It's definitely not map coordinates.
What do you think?

292
00:14:46,007 --> 00:14:50,303
- Well...
- 752 598.

293
00:14:50,637 --> 00:14:51,930
A street address, maybe.

294
00:14:53,807 --> 00:14:54,849
Too many numbers.

295
00:14:55,475 --> 00:14:56,518
Yes.

296
00:14:56,851 --> 00:14:58,686
Uh... or what about a date?

297
00:14:58,978 --> 00:15:03,066
Like 752 would be July 1952.

298
00:15:04,025 --> 00:15:05,068
Maybe. But why?

299
00:15:12,409 --> 00:15:13,451
They're out back.

300
00:15:16,204 --> 00:15:18,373
Exhale, Warrior II.

301
00:15:19,749 --> 00:15:22,293
Beautiful.
Now flow through your vinyasa.

302
00:15:24,587 --> 00:15:25,839
Excuse me. Monica Giraldi?

303
00:15:28,508 --> 00:15:29,551
Yes.

304
00:15:30,135 --> 00:15:31,886
Kimball Cho, FBI.
This is Agent Fischer.

305
00:15:33,138 --> 00:15:34,597
You were Greta De Jorio's
business partner?

306
00:15:35,056 --> 00:15:37,600
Well, a friend more than
partner, but... yes.

307
00:15:37,934 --> 00:15:39,686
Why? What's happened?
Have you caught someone?

308
00:15:41,688 --> 00:15:42,730
Oh, my God.

309
00:15:46,025 --> 00:15:47,110
Everything okay, Monica?

310
00:15:47,527 --> 00:15:49,362
Yes. Thank you, Tanya.
I'm sorry.

311
00:15:50,738 --> 00:15:52,991
It's just brought that whole terrible
time flooding back.

312
00:15:54,659 --> 00:15:58,705
Greta and I were friends at college.
We were... close, like close.

313
00:15:59,706 --> 00:16:01,583
We, uh, both loved interior design

314
00:16:01,708 --> 00:16:04,043
so going into business together
was a no-brainer.

315
00:16:04,669 --> 00:16:07,505
Our firm started taking off
as she died.

316
00:16:07,630 --> 00:16:11,926
I mean, I continue to run the firm
but it wasn't the same.

317
00:16:12,051 --> 00:16:14,596
She was such a joyous person.

318
00:16:17,098 --> 00:16:19,601
What was her state of mind
in the days leading up to her death?

319
00:16:20,059 --> 00:16:22,103
Did she express any anxieties or fears?

320
00:16:25,356 --> 00:16:28,693
I never told anyone this
but a few months before she died

321
00:16:28,818 --> 00:16:31,446
she told me she was having an affair...

322
00:16:32,363 --> 00:16:35,033
That it was real love,
and she'd never been happier.

323
00:16:35,533 --> 00:16:37,952
Why didn't you tell
the investigators at the time?

324
00:16:38,286 --> 00:16:39,704
I didn't wanna make her look bad.

325
00:16:39,913 --> 00:16:41,748
- Did she give you a name?
- No.

326
00:16:42,123 --> 00:16:45,168
I begged, of course, but she wouldn't.

327
00:16:46,085 --> 00:16:47,295
She never mentioned him again

328
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:50,548
so I figured the affair
ended badly, you know?

329
00:16:51,508 --> 00:16:52,550
They usually do.

330
00:16:55,386 --> 00:16:56,429
Real...

331
00:16:57,972 --> 00:16:59,015
Real what?

332
00:17:01,017 --> 00:17:02,393
Jane, could you turn that down?

333
00:17:04,103 --> 00:17:06,147
I'm sorry, Cho. I couldn't hear you.
What was that?

334
00:17:08,233 --> 00:17:10,151
Okay. Got it.
See you later.

335
00:17:11,402 --> 00:17:13,071
Greta was having an affair.

336
00:17:13,363 --> 00:17:14,405
With who, we don't know.

337
00:17:15,031 --> 00:17:18,451
An affair. Of course. Well,
that's relationships for you, isn't it?

338
00:17:19,035 --> 00:17:21,079
They always seem to end up
in a tragic web

339
00:17:21,204 --> 00:17:22,872
of deceit and betrayal, don't they?

340
00:17:23,456 --> 00:17:26,167
The ones we see? Of course they do.
Stop it.

341
00:17:26,292 --> 00:17:28,169
- Stop what?
- Trying to convince me not to leave.

342
00:17:28,294 --> 00:17:29,337
Oh, come on. I know better than that.

343
00:17:29,462 --> 00:17:30,838
Once you've made your mind up,
it's made up.

344
00:17:31,422 --> 00:17:32,840
- For better or for worse...
- Shush.

345
00:17:32,966 --> 00:17:35,301
- Richer or poorer.
- Seriously? Not funny.

346
00:17:37,053 --> 00:17:38,137
Wesley Baxter.

347
00:17:46,938 --> 00:17:48,731
- You, you think this is me?
- No.

348
00:17:49,732 --> 00:17:51,776
- I-I swear to God...
- Relax. It's okay.

349
00:17:51,901 --> 00:17:54,237
We, we can tell
that you've never seen this before.

350
00:17:55,154 --> 00:17:56,197
Relax?

351
00:17:56,614 --> 00:17:58,199
You people destroyed my life.

352
00:17:58,908 --> 00:18:00,451
I mean, my own parents
thought I was guilty.

353
00:18:00,702 --> 00:18:04,414
You were a legit suspect. The file
says that you were stalking Greta.

354
00:18:04,581 --> 00:18:05,748
You were spying on her.

355
00:18:06,124 --> 00:18:07,875
I-I didn't. I wasn't...

356
00:18:08,835 --> 00:18:09,877
stalking Greta.

357
00:18:11,337 --> 00:18:14,215
- Of course.
- What?

358
00:18:14,924 --> 00:18:15,967
You were spying on Christie.

359
00:18:17,176 --> 00:18:18,469
No. I...

360
00:18:22,473 --> 00:18:23,516
I mean...

361
00:18:24,350 --> 00:18:25,393
Kind of.

362
00:18:26,728 --> 00:18:28,146
I was watching Christie.

363
00:18:29,647 --> 00:18:30,690
I was in love with her.

364
00:18:32,567 --> 00:18:33,610
I was just a stupid kid.

365
00:18:34,152 --> 00:18:36,154
Why didn't you say anything
about this then?

366
00:18:37,697 --> 00:18:39,949
Oh, sure. That would've made
everything all right.

367
00:18:41,159 --> 00:18:42,952
I mean, she was 15.
I was 18.

368
00:18:43,494 --> 00:18:44,537
You do the math.

369
00:18:44,871 --> 00:18:46,998
Well, for what it's worth,
we talked to Christie

370
00:18:47,123 --> 00:18:48,458
and she always thought
you were innocent.

371
00:18:49,459 --> 00:18:50,501
She did?

372
00:18:51,502 --> 00:18:53,212
- She said that?
- Yeah, she did.

373
00:18:55,298 --> 00:18:57,216
Does that code mean anything to you?

374
00:19:01,554 --> 00:19:03,848
- Nope.
- Okay, well, you keep that copy

375
00:19:03,973 --> 00:19:06,684
and if anything, anything comes
to mind, you let us know, huh?

376
00:19:07,477 --> 00:19:09,103
- Okay.
- Thanks for your time.

377
00:19:22,283 --> 00:19:23,409
I think he's telling the truth.

378
00:19:24,243 --> 00:19:25,286
I agree.

379
00:19:26,954 --> 00:19:29,707
Let's go to the beach,
take a little walk.

380
00:19:30,291 --> 00:19:31,334
It's close to here.

381
00:19:31,751 --> 00:19:34,045
- We're working.
- Oh, come on. Live a little.

382
00:19:40,176 --> 00:19:41,219
Well, this is nice.

383
00:19:43,596 --> 00:19:45,556
We don't take enough time
to do stuff like this.

384
00:19:45,765 --> 00:19:47,183
That's because we're wasting time.

385
00:19:47,725 --> 00:19:50,061
Well, okay. Let's solve
that code, then, shall we?

386
00:19:50,186 --> 00:19:51,229
Just like that, huh?

387
00:19:54,190 --> 00:19:55,817
"Ask yourselves...
do you understand."

388
00:19:55,942 --> 00:19:58,820
Well, that's an odd turn of phrase.
Must have some hidden meaning.

389
00:19:58,945 --> 00:20:00,947
Ask yourselves...
do we understand?

390
00:20:02,156 --> 00:20:03,408
Do we understand?

391
00:20:04,409 --> 00:20:05,993
Do we? Do we understand?

392
00:20:06,119 --> 00:20:07,787
- Dewey.
- Do we? That's what I said.

393
00:20:08,079 --> 00:20:09,997
- The Dewey decimal system.
- Oh!

394
00:20:10,665 --> 00:20:14,252
That's what the numbers stand for.
It's a way of cataloging library books.

395
00:20:14,377 --> 00:20:17,672
Oh. 752 598.

396
00:20:18,172 --> 00:20:23,720
752 is color
and 598 means bird.

397
00:20:24,178 --> 00:20:26,139
Color bird, what does that mean?

398
00:20:26,347 --> 00:20:28,391
There's no number for specific colors

399
00:20:28,516 --> 00:20:31,269
so it's blue bird, red bird, whatever.

400
00:20:31,394 --> 00:20:34,522
H, H, the radio call sign
for H is hotel.

401
00:20:35,982 --> 00:20:38,693
There's a Blue Bird Lodge
on the Islamorada in the Keys.

402
00:20:38,901 --> 00:20:39,944
"Is M Key."

403
00:20:40,570 --> 00:20:42,447
Islamorada, Key.

404
00:20:42,989 --> 00:20:44,407
Lisbon, you solved it.

405
00:20:44,657 --> 00:20:46,075
- I did, didn't I?
- You did.

406
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:48,578
- But what does the O at the end mean?
- Oh. Uh... It's a date.

407
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:51,205
- O is a date?
- O, it's the astronomic symbol

408
00:20:51,330 --> 00:20:52,707
for, uh, the full moon.

409
00:20:53,875 --> 00:20:55,251
It's a full moon tomorrow night.

410
00:20:56,294 --> 00:20:57,336
You just cracked the case.

411
00:20:57,920 --> 00:20:59,797
- We cracked it.
- If you asked nicely

412
00:20:59,922 --> 00:21:03,342
I would bet that Abbott will let you go
back to Washington right now.

413
00:21:03,468 --> 00:21:05,386
Are you kidding me?
I wouldn't miss this for the world.

414
00:21:05,511 --> 00:21:07,847
I'm gonna be on the Islamorada
tomorrow night.

415
00:21:09,182 --> 00:21:11,267
- I did it. I did it.
- You did it.

416
00:21:11,434 --> 00:21:12,769
I did it this time, not you.

417
00:21:13,436 --> 00:21:15,855
- Who did it? It was me. I did it.
- You did it.

418
00:21:44,423 --> 00:21:45,549
"Welcome to the Blue Bird."

419
00:21:45,883 --> 00:21:49,011
You have adjoining waterside suites
just as you requested, Mr. Jane.

420
00:21:49,220 --> 00:21:51,138
- Thank you.
- Sounds expensive.

421
00:21:51,263 --> 00:21:53,974
Well, why not?
You know, it could be our last, uh...

422
00:21:55,434 --> 00:21:56,477
Hurrah.

423
00:21:56,602 --> 00:21:59,355
Someone may call the hotel
asking for a Greta De Jorio.

424
00:21:59,480 --> 00:22:01,565
Anyone that does that,
give them my room number.

425
00:22:01,982 --> 00:22:03,901
The room number, but not my name.

426
00:22:04,109 --> 00:22:06,695
And, uh, tell the night staff
to do the same, yes?

427
00:22:06,862 --> 00:22:08,030
- Certainly, sir.
- Thank you.

428
00:22:08,280 --> 00:22:10,157
- Just your room number, De Jorio.
- Perfect.

429
00:22:10,616 --> 00:22:12,326
Oh, would you like me to confirm
your dinner reservation?

430
00:22:12,451 --> 00:22:13,994
- Yes, ma'am.
- Certainly.

431
00:22:14,119 --> 00:22:16,330
Just so you know, um,
evening attire is preferred.

432
00:22:16,455 --> 00:22:18,582
I'll, I'll just get a sandwich
or order room service.

433
00:22:18,707 --> 00:22:21,085
- I don't have anything to wear.
- Oh, we'll figure it out. Thank you.

434
00:22:21,210 --> 00:22:22,711
It's right up the stairs on the first floor.

435
00:22:22,837 --> 00:22:23,879
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

436
00:22:40,104 --> 00:22:41,146
Whoa.

437
00:22:48,654 --> 00:22:49,697
Oh, my God.

438
00:22:57,246 --> 00:22:58,289
Beautiful.

439
00:23:12,177 --> 00:23:13,220
Hey.

440
00:23:13,679 --> 00:23:16,974
Jane, this is too much.
It's too nice. We're on a case.

441
00:23:17,182 --> 00:23:19,435
Well, it might be our last case
together, so, uh...

442
00:23:20,519 --> 00:23:22,021
How could anything be too nice?

443
00:23:22,563 --> 00:23:23,898
The dresses are beautiful.

444
00:23:24,523 --> 00:23:25,566
I'll see you at dinner.

445
00:23:38,329 --> 00:23:39,371
Hello?

446
00:23:40,664 --> 00:23:42,041
Huh? Yeah.

447
00:23:43,208 --> 00:23:45,085
Here? Now?

448
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:48,714
Hey, Jane. Nice place.
Our unsub has good taste, huh?

449
00:23:48,839 --> 00:23:50,341
Yeah. W-what are you guys
doing here?

450
00:23:50,466 --> 00:23:51,842
Lisbon and I can handle this.

451
00:23:51,967 --> 00:23:55,012
Well, this is a big case
with a whole lotta downside potential.

452
00:23:55,137 --> 00:23:57,264
D.C. wants to put senior agents
on the scene.

453
00:23:58,349 --> 00:23:59,600
Why? Do you have a problem
with that?

454
00:24:00,684 --> 00:24:02,478
Nope. No problem.

455
00:24:03,145 --> 00:24:04,605
I'm starving. How's the food here?

456
00:24:06,273 --> 00:24:07,566
Yeah, apparently, it's good.

457
00:24:17,368 --> 00:24:18,410
No burgers.

458
00:24:20,287 --> 00:24:21,580
You know, I wish my wife were here.

459
00:24:22,414 --> 00:24:23,749
Women really love this kinda stuff.

460
00:24:24,083 --> 00:24:25,250
Yeah, they do.

461
00:24:42,601 --> 00:24:44,186
Hi. I was wondering...

462
00:24:44,311 --> 00:24:46,981
My boyfriend would love
the bathrobe in the room.

463
00:24:47,106 --> 00:24:49,316
- Could I buy one?
- Oh, there's no need, ma'am.

464
00:24:49,441 --> 00:24:50,901
The package Mr. Jane booked
last week

465
00:24:51,026 --> 00:24:52,611
actually includes
a complimentary robe.

466
00:24:52,736 --> 00:24:53,779
That's great.

467
00:24:56,407 --> 00:24:57,449
Wait. Um...

468
00:24:58,450 --> 00:24:59,660
Last week?

469
00:25:01,120 --> 00:25:02,830
He booked the room last week?

470
00:25:03,539 --> 00:25:04,581
Yes, ma'am.

471
00:25:07,209 --> 00:25:09,169
- The wine list, sir.
- Oh, no, thank you.

472
00:25:11,296 --> 00:25:14,174
Teresa.
Wow. You look spiffy.

473
00:25:16,635 --> 00:25:18,804
Tell me something...
how is it possible

474
00:25:18,929 --> 00:25:21,056
that you booked the rooms here
last week

475
00:25:21,181 --> 00:25:23,183
two days before the letter
even arrived?

476
00:25:26,729 --> 00:25:28,355
Okay, um...

477
00:25:29,064 --> 00:25:30,107
You got me.

478
00:25:30,566 --> 00:25:32,276
I wrote the letter. It was me.

479
00:25:32,401 --> 00:25:35,612
It was, uh, just a ruse,
but, uh, I'm 100% confident

480
00:25:35,738 --> 00:25:37,072
that it's gonna smoke out the killer.

481
00:25:37,865 --> 00:25:39,116
You son of a bitch!

482
00:25:55,299 --> 00:25:56,717
Maybe I should, um...

483
00:25:58,093 --> 00:25:59,136
go and talk to her.

484
00:26:00,262 --> 00:26:01,305
Yeah.

485
00:26:03,891 --> 00:26:04,933
Uh...

486
00:26:06,977 --> 00:26:09,897
Probably should order without us.

487
00:26:11,106 --> 00:26:12,149
Yeah. Okay.

488
00:26:14,234 --> 00:26:16,528
Hey, you know, I will take
a look at that wine list.

489
00:26:18,614 --> 00:26:19,656
Thank you.

490
00:26:24,119 --> 00:26:26,288
I need a taxi to the airport right now.

491
00:26:27,831 --> 00:26:28,874
Okay. Thank you.

492
00:26:43,722 --> 00:26:44,765
"Lisbon?"

493
00:26:45,224 --> 00:26:46,266
Go away!

494
00:26:47,851 --> 00:26:48,894
Please open the door.

495
00:26:50,479 --> 00:26:51,522
Go away.

496
00:26:53,816 --> 00:26:56,735
You know, I'm...
I'm sorry that I tricked you.

497
00:26:56,860 --> 00:26:57,903
I just...

498
00:26:58,987 --> 00:27:00,155
I just, uh...

499
00:27:01,448 --> 00:27:03,575
You just what?
Jane, what were you doing?

500
00:27:06,703 --> 00:27:08,163
I just don't want you to leave.

501
00:27:10,165 --> 00:27:12,960
You don't give a damn
about what I want or need.

502
00:27:13,085 --> 00:27:15,546
I am just a convenience for you.
You used me.

503
00:27:15,838 --> 00:27:17,047
"It's all about you."

504
00:27:19,299 --> 00:27:21,260
You used a-a woman's murder, Jane.

505
00:27:21,385 --> 00:27:24,304
You basically dug her corpse up
for this crap.

506
00:27:25,472 --> 00:27:27,891
You are so twisted up
in your own dishonesty

507
00:27:28,016 --> 00:27:31,687
"you have no idea how to act
like a decent human being."

508
00:27:31,937 --> 00:27:32,980
No idea.

509
00:27:35,816 --> 00:27:37,693
I'm sorry, okay?

510
00:27:39,194 --> 00:27:40,237
I'm sorry.

511
00:27:41,530 --> 00:27:44,992
All right, well, if you're really sorry
why don't you just leave me alone?

512
00:27:47,411 --> 00:27:48,453
"Go away!"

513
00:27:53,834 --> 00:27:55,711
I know you're there.
I can hear you breathing.

514
00:27:56,712 --> 00:27:58,005
Just leave me alone.

515
00:28:23,864 --> 00:28:26,533
- Hey, it's me.
- Hey, sweetheart, how are you?

516
00:28:26,700 --> 00:28:29,328
Good. Good. Um...

517
00:28:31,997 --> 00:28:35,042
Listen, if the offer still stands,
the answer is yes.

518
00:28:35,876 --> 00:28:36,919
Let's get married.

519
00:28:37,628 --> 00:28:38,670
Yes!

520
00:28:38,962 --> 00:28:41,673
Good. Good. Well, I'm...
I'm on my way to D.C. now.

521
00:28:42,132 --> 00:28:45,385
Okay. All right, all right.
Text me your flight information

522
00:28:45,510 --> 00:28:46,845
"and I'll be there."

523
00:28:48,555 --> 00:28:49,848
"Teresa, it's gonna be great."

524
00:28:50,182 --> 00:28:51,767
"You're gonna love the neighborhood."

525
00:28:51,892 --> 00:28:54,686
"It's, it's full of restaurants,
your favorite kind."

526
00:28:54,811 --> 00:28:56,521
"I got a number to an excellent broker"

527
00:28:56,647 --> 00:28:59,733
"and, apparently, he's got some
appointments set up for us."

528
00:29:05,612 --> 00:29:08,198
He stood in my office,
looked me in the eye

529
00:29:08,323 --> 00:29:09,741
and lied to me, and I bought it.

530
00:29:10,158 --> 00:29:12,118
Don't feel bad.
Jane could sell cats to mice.

531
00:29:13,912 --> 00:29:15,288
I hope he's fixed things
with Lisbon, though.

532
00:29:15,538 --> 00:29:16,581
Yeah.

533
00:29:17,207 --> 00:29:18,667
- Think we should go check on him?
- No.

534
00:29:21,294 --> 00:29:22,337
It's weird how mad she got.

535
00:29:23,255 --> 00:29:24,631
I mean, he's pulled way worse stunts
than this.

536
00:29:25,173 --> 00:29:27,467
He buried a man alive once.
She wasn't nearly as angry as this.

537
00:29:32,013 --> 00:29:33,056
Really?

538
00:29:34,808 --> 00:29:35,850
You don't know why?

539
00:29:36,810 --> 00:29:37,852
Don't know why what?

540
00:29:46,945 --> 00:29:50,907
Flight 407 to Washington, D.C.
will begin boarding in 45 minutes.

541
00:29:51,366 --> 00:29:52,826
"We apologize for the delay."

542
00:29:53,076 --> 00:29:55,578
"If there's anyone who has any hand
baggage they'd like to check"

543
00:29:55,787 --> 00:29:57,122
"please come see me at the desk."

544
00:30:21,146 --> 00:30:23,356
You're the FBI man, Jane.

545
00:30:24,524 --> 00:30:27,193
Oh, you're the lawyer,
uh, Randolph, right?

546
00:30:27,652 --> 00:30:28,737
Yeah, yeah, that's right.

547
00:30:29,529 --> 00:30:31,072
I don't understand what's going on here.

548
00:30:31,448 --> 00:30:32,699
You killed Greta De Jorio?

549
00:30:32,991 --> 00:30:34,784
What? No, I didn't kill Greta.

550
00:30:35,618 --> 00:30:36,661
Yeah.

551
00:30:37,620 --> 00:30:38,663
Oh!

552
00:30:38,913 --> 00:30:40,040
You're her secret lover.

553
00:30:41,249 --> 00:30:42,292
How...

554
00:30:45,670 --> 00:30:46,713
Yes.

555
00:30:47,213 --> 00:30:48,840
I solved the clue in the letter
and I came here

556
00:30:48,965 --> 00:30:50,550
to kill the bastard that killed Greta.

557
00:30:52,260 --> 00:30:53,303
That's so romantic.

558
00:30:53,803 --> 00:30:55,764
Uh, do-do you mind putting
the gun down, please?

559
00:30:58,725 --> 00:31:00,769
Did...
Did you catch him already?

560
00:31:01,102 --> 00:31:02,145
Yeah, no. I...

561
00:31:02,979 --> 00:31:04,564
I-I wrote the letter, not the killer.

562
00:31:05,065 --> 00:31:07,400
- What?
- Yeah...

563
00:31:07,734 --> 00:31:10,987
It's a long, sad story, but don't worry.

564
00:31:11,112 --> 00:31:13,573
The killer will show up.
Well, that's the plan, at least.

565
00:31:13,698 --> 00:31:14,741
Can I get you a drink?

566
00:31:16,451 --> 00:31:17,494
Yeah, sure.

567
00:31:17,702 --> 00:31:19,329
Vodka, rum, gin, or whiskey?

568
00:31:19,704 --> 00:31:21,247
Uh... Gin.

569
00:31:22,707 --> 00:31:23,750
Hands up.

570
00:31:25,960 --> 00:31:27,504
Oh. Wes Baxter.

571
00:31:29,297 --> 00:31:31,716
- Hi. Welcome.
- You.

572
00:31:32,926 --> 00:31:34,594
W-what the hell?
Who's this?

573
00:31:34,761 --> 00:31:36,971
- Is this him? Is this the killer?
- No, no. He's not the killer.

574
00:31:37,097 --> 00:31:39,307
And, uh, you're not the killer
either, are you?

575
00:31:40,183 --> 00:31:41,726
- No.
- No. Didn't think so.

576
00:31:42,519 --> 00:31:44,229
Uh, you mind putting the gun down?

577
00:31:46,314 --> 00:31:47,357
Thank you.

578
00:31:47,774 --> 00:31:49,401
You came to seek revenge, too, huh?

579
00:31:50,610 --> 00:31:51,986
I shouldn't have made
the clue so easy.

580
00:31:53,571 --> 00:31:56,741
Whoever killed Mrs. De Jorio...
he ruined my life.

581
00:31:57,158 --> 00:32:00,453
Uh... vodka, rum, gin, or whiskey?

582
00:32:02,122 --> 00:32:03,164
Rum.

583
00:32:03,540 --> 00:32:05,083
Greta and I were deeply in love.

584
00:32:06,709 --> 00:32:08,962
She was gonna divorce Don
and marry me.

585
00:32:11,631 --> 00:32:12,674
I called it off.

586
00:32:14,342 --> 00:32:15,385
I ended the affair.

587
00:32:19,013 --> 00:32:22,976
I was afraid of the scandal and the damage
it would do to my business.

588
00:32:24,310 --> 00:32:25,353
I was a coward.

589
00:32:26,563 --> 00:32:28,440
Blind, stupid coward.

590
00:32:29,649 --> 00:32:33,027
I was so filled with fear and self-hatred

591
00:32:33,153 --> 00:32:35,780
that I destroyed the best thing
that ever happened to me.

592
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:38,950
By the time I realized my mistake...

593
00:32:40,743 --> 00:32:42,287
it was too late, she was gone.

594
00:32:44,456 --> 00:32:45,498
I'm sorry, man.

595
00:32:47,375 --> 00:32:48,418
Bummer.

596
00:32:54,549 --> 00:32:55,592
I have to go to the airport.

597
00:33:00,221 --> 00:33:01,264
What's his deal?

598
00:33:02,223 --> 00:33:03,266
You gotta be kidding me.

599
00:33:03,892 --> 00:33:04,934
Turn around.

600
00:33:06,269 --> 00:33:07,312
Hands up.

601
00:33:08,188 --> 00:33:10,440
- Put your hands up.
- You must be Monica?

602
00:33:11,941 --> 00:33:14,360
- I'm kind of in a hurry here.
- Who are you people?

603
00:33:15,653 --> 00:33:16,779
And why did you send that letter?

604
00:33:17,489 --> 00:33:18,531
Uh...

605
00:33:20,366 --> 00:33:22,660
I know you.
You're the lawyer, Randolph.

606
00:33:23,161 --> 00:33:24,204
Got old, huh?

607
00:33:26,372 --> 00:33:27,957
And you. You're the kid
from next door.

608
00:33:28,082 --> 00:33:29,125
So, who are you?

609
00:33:29,542 --> 00:33:31,419
Okay, I-I-I don't want you
to be alarmed

610
00:33:31,544 --> 00:33:33,171
but I'm, uh, FBI.

611
00:33:33,671 --> 00:33:35,840
If you leave now
I will bet that you get away

612
00:33:35,965 --> 00:33:39,385
before my colleagues get here
but you have to leave right now.

613
00:33:39,928 --> 00:33:40,970
You're lying.

614
00:33:41,930 --> 00:33:42,972
You're not a fed.

615
00:33:43,389 --> 00:33:46,476
I-I know that I don't look like one
but I assure you I am one.

616
00:33:46,851 --> 00:33:47,894
Show me your badge.

617
00:33:49,103 --> 00:33:51,189
Okay. There we go.

618
00:33:51,856 --> 00:33:52,899
Oh, I'm sorry.

619
00:33:55,902 --> 00:33:58,321
Damn.
He is. He's FBI.

620
00:33:59,072 --> 00:34:01,699
I told you this was a setup. I told you.
Damn it, Monica!

621
00:34:01,908 --> 00:34:03,243
Okay, just calm down.

622
00:34:04,077 --> 00:34:06,704
If there were more FBI around
they'd be here by now, wouldn't they?

623
00:34:07,830 --> 00:34:08,873
It's just him.

624
00:34:10,667 --> 00:34:12,252
I don't see it.
They're like brother and sister.

625
00:34:13,503 --> 00:34:14,546
There's no way.

626
00:34:17,549 --> 00:34:19,759
Oh, speak of the devil,
a text from Jane.

627
00:34:21,052 --> 00:34:22,220
"SOZ."

628
00:34:23,805 --> 00:34:24,847
SOZ.

629
00:34:26,391 --> 00:34:27,517
- S.O.S.
- Oh.

630
00:34:29,018 --> 00:34:30,979
- So, what do we do?
- I'm thinking.

631
00:34:31,521 --> 00:34:33,773
Listen, ladies, all you've done so far
is wave a gun around.

632
00:34:33,898 --> 00:34:35,149
That's no big deal, huh?

633
00:34:35,817 --> 00:34:38,152
- No one's calling you killers.
- I am.

634
00:34:41,114 --> 00:34:42,490
No one can prove anything, can they?

635
00:34:44,284 --> 00:34:46,995
You walk away now
and let your lawyers fix this.

636
00:34:47,453 --> 00:34:48,496
He's right.

637
00:34:48,830 --> 00:34:49,872
What else can we do?

638
00:34:50,456 --> 00:34:52,083
We can just walk away?

639
00:34:52,292 --> 00:34:54,711
Yeah, but you have to go right now
before my colleagues arrive.

640
00:34:54,836 --> 00:34:56,296
Before anyone gets hurt...
go.

641
00:34:57,422 --> 00:34:58,798
- Okay, let's go.
- Attagirl.

642
00:34:59,632 --> 00:35:01,467
- Stay right where you are.
- Oh, dude.

643
00:35:02,302 --> 00:35:03,344
Put your hands up.

644
00:35:05,346 --> 00:35:06,389
Do it.

645
00:35:10,476 --> 00:35:11,519
Now put the gun down.

646
00:35:25,867 --> 00:35:26,909
Don't move!

647
00:35:27,660 --> 00:35:29,120
Ow! My ears!

648
00:35:30,622 --> 00:35:33,166
- That is loud!
- Oh, you're fine.

649
00:35:33,291 --> 00:35:35,960
And, uh, yeah, you're fine.
It's just a flesh wound, okay?

650
00:35:36,085 --> 00:35:39,631
I have to go, but help
will be here momentarily, okay?

651
00:35:39,797 --> 00:35:40,923
Jane, what the hell?

652
00:35:41,049 --> 00:35:43,217
You have a car with a siren, right?
I need your car keys.

653
00:35:43,343 --> 00:35:44,677
- What?
- I need your car keys.

654
00:35:44,802 --> 00:35:46,304
I gotta get to the airport.
It's an emergency.

655
00:35:46,429 --> 00:35:48,431
- Where's Lisbon?
- She's at the airport.

656
00:35:49,223 --> 00:35:50,725
- Oh, I see.
- Thank you.

657
00:35:50,850 --> 00:35:52,352
Hey, hey, hey. You wanna
give us some guidance?

658
00:35:52,477 --> 00:35:54,979
Yes. Uh, those two are the bad guys.
They're the good guys.

659
00:35:55,104 --> 00:35:56,648
And blondie's an embezzler, I suspect.

660
00:35:57,190 --> 00:35:58,232
- Hey, Cho.
- Hey.

661
00:35:59,275 --> 00:36:00,818
- Thanks. Good luck.
- Where's he going?

662
00:36:19,835 --> 00:36:22,046
"This is Lisbon.
Leave a message. I'll call you back."

663
00:36:22,380 --> 00:36:25,007
Uh, Lisbon. It's...
it's Jane.

664
00:36:25,716 --> 00:36:28,886
If you get this, call me, please.
Please?

665
00:37:05,381 --> 00:37:07,925
Sorry. FBI, FBI.
Uh, you gotta let me through.

666
00:37:08,300 --> 00:37:10,636
- ID?
- Yeah. Uh...

667
00:37:11,846 --> 00:37:12,888
Oh.

668
00:37:13,305 --> 00:37:15,850
Uh, it's in my car.
My car is out the front.

669
00:37:15,975 --> 00:37:17,601
It's a federal vehicle out the front.

670
00:37:22,148 --> 00:37:23,190
Next.

671
00:37:51,427 --> 00:37:54,013
Wait! Wait!
Hang on!

672
00:37:55,055 --> 00:37:57,349
Wait!
Hang on a second!

673
00:38:03,647 --> 00:38:06,317
Hello! Hello! Let me in?

674
00:38:06,650 --> 00:38:07,693
Let me in, please.

675
00:38:11,447 --> 00:38:13,365
- Yes?
- Thank you. FBI. Won't be a moment.

676
00:38:13,491 --> 00:38:14,950
- FBI? What's wrong?
- Uh, yeah, nothing.

677
00:38:15,075 --> 00:38:16,869
Nothing to worry about.
Just a routine check.

678
00:38:24,543 --> 00:38:26,754
- There you are.
- What are you doing here?

679
00:38:27,463 --> 00:38:30,299
- There's something I need to say.
- I don't wanna see you. Go away.

680
00:38:30,591 --> 00:38:32,009
You're right. You're right.

681
00:38:32,134 --> 00:38:34,804
I-I have forgotten how to act
like a normal human being.

682
00:38:35,304 --> 00:38:38,766
And I play games, I lie and I...
and I trick people

683
00:38:39,433 --> 00:38:41,435
to avoid the truth of how I feel.

684
00:38:42,812 --> 00:38:44,271
And the idea of letting anyone
close to me

685
00:38:44,396 --> 00:38:46,774
is-is-is terrifying,
for obvious reasons.

686
00:38:47,358 --> 00:38:50,694
But the truth, Teresa is that...

687
00:38:51,487 --> 00:38:55,032
I can't imagine waking up
knowing that I won't see you.

688
00:38:59,787 --> 00:39:00,830
The truth is...

689
00:39:02,039 --> 00:39:03,082
I love you.

690
00:39:07,336 --> 00:39:09,129
You can't imagine how good
that feels to say out loud

691
00:39:09,255 --> 00:39:13,008
but it scares me...
and it is the truth.

692
00:39:13,425 --> 00:39:15,511
It is the truth of what I feel.

693
00:39:17,513 --> 00:39:19,974
It's too late.
Jane, it's too late.

694
00:39:20,432 --> 00:39:23,060
Maybe. Maybe and I understand.
That's okay.

695
00:39:23,185 --> 00:39:26,063
I needed to get to this,
and you deserve to hear it.

696
00:39:26,313 --> 00:39:27,565
Put your hands in the air right now.
Do it!

697
00:39:28,190 --> 00:39:29,358
I love you, Teresa.

698
00:39:31,569 --> 00:39:33,612
And it makes me happy to be able
to say that to you.

699
00:39:35,072 --> 00:39:36,115
I love her.

700
00:39:36,365 --> 00:39:38,158
That woman in 12B...
I love her.

701
00:39:38,534 --> 00:39:39,618
You take care of her.

702
00:39:40,661 --> 00:39:43,706
- I'm sorry. That was embarrassing.
- Oh, shush, honey.

703
00:39:44,081 --> 00:39:46,709
Every woman on this plane
is green with envy.

704
00:39:55,593 --> 00:39:57,678
The D.A. will press
for second degree and a plea

705
00:39:57,803 --> 00:40:00,180
but I say we bid high
with murder one.

706
00:40:00,931 --> 00:40:02,641
Okay. Why don't you take care
of processing?

707
00:40:02,766 --> 00:40:04,268
I have to go to the airport

708
00:40:04,393 --> 00:40:06,645
and try to persuade TSA to let Jane go.

709
00:40:07,396 --> 00:40:08,439
He's still in custody?

710
00:40:08,981 --> 00:40:11,567
They do not like people
messing with their airplanes.

711
00:40:12,234 --> 00:40:13,319
Don't like it at all.

712
00:40:46,936 --> 00:40:47,978
Hey.

713
00:40:49,396 --> 00:40:50,439
Hi.

714
00:40:51,607 --> 00:40:54,693
This is another fine pickle
you've gotten yourself in, huh?

715
00:40:54,860 --> 00:40:56,904
Eh, I've seen worse, pickle-wise.

716
00:40:57,488 --> 00:40:58,530
Yes, you have.

717
00:41:00,616 --> 00:41:02,368
- How's the ankle?
- Oh, it's fine.

718
00:41:04,578 --> 00:41:05,704
You didn't go to D.C.

719
00:41:06,747 --> 00:41:07,790
No.

720
00:41:12,753 --> 00:41:14,004
Did you mean what you said?

721
00:41:15,172 --> 00:41:17,716
- Yes, I did.
- Good.

722
00:41:19,218 --> 00:41:21,553
Just to be clear, we're talking
about pickles, right?

723
00:41:22,596 --> 00:41:24,682
No. No.
The other thing.

724
00:41:25,391 --> 00:41:26,642
Oh, that.

725
00:41:28,018 --> 00:41:29,520
This is no joking matter.

726
00:41:30,521 --> 00:41:31,563
Yes.

727
00:41:32,314 --> 00:41:34,191
I meant what I said, every word of it.

728
00:41:35,067 --> 00:41:36,110
Good.

729
00:41:37,277 --> 00:41:38,737
Because I feel the same way.

730
00:41:40,739 --> 00:41:41,782
Well, that's lucky.

731
00:41:46,787 --> 00:41:47,830
What about Pike?

732
00:41:49,623 --> 00:41:50,666
He'll understand.

733
00:41:55,671 --> 00:41:58,048
- Say it again.
- Say what again?

734
00:42:13,063 --> 00:42:14,481
Hey, quit that!


